# Taking a lot of time off work
If you've been keeping up, in my [last blog post](/blog/posts/what_the_flock.html) I mentioned that at the start of december I drastically cut my work hours down. I now work on 10-14 hours/week, spread out over 2-3 days. The cut is so drastic that even with my supportive partner paying a higher % into rent then me I'll still financially be in the red month after month. I'm planning to keep to bleed money for the next 2-6 months.

## How did I get here?
I won't go into the deep details, but I've had a bad 3 or 4 years mental health wise. One day when I was visiting a mall I had a sudden and intense panic attack, something that I had never experienced before. After that day, I suddenly developed an intense [agoraphobia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agoraphobia).

At most times of the day I was managing some sort of strong anxiety or panic; I had to work very hard to distract myself from it. I found that going outside my apartment made things much worse, especially if I was in a noisy/crowded space. I also couldn't go to sleep unless I was completely physically exhausted, I had some sort of panic instinct that would prevent me from willingly giving up consciousness for any reason. I ended up with a wacky ever shifting sleep schedule that involved staying up 18+ hours often as a result. For the first couple of months it was extremely difficult to deal with. As time went on, I slowly got better at managing it.

Thankfully, I worked a remote job at the time. I did software development for a small startup company and for the most part I didn't have any required times I had to be online. Because I got to work when I wanted, I got to exploit the bits of time during my day when I could focus in order to get my daily work done. I'm forever grateful I got to work that job during that time, I don't think any other job could have accommodated my struggles quite so seamlessly. Not only that, but they paid me fairly well given my low amount of previous professional experience.

The startup company I worked for at the time was pre-revenue and was running low on funds. The founder started looking everywhere they could for outside investment; I could see the writing on the wall. In order to make sure the company could continue operating, the founder had to make the decision to lay off almost all the employees (including me).

I had no idea what had triggered my original panic attack that caused such intense regression. In the back of my mind I had a worry that one day another huge panic attack would hit me and reverse all of my progress I had made. Because of that concern, I had been focused on saving money. The idea was, if I had a less accommodating job in the future, I could quit and ride on savings for an extended time if I had a regression again in the future.

As soon as I learned my contract with the startup company would be terminated, I started looking for the first job I could get as a temporary in-between job. I wanted to protect my savings as fiercely as possible and minimize any gap in employment. I got a job at a retail shipping store just in time to have a zero day gap in employment. Shipping is sort of one of my "things" so to say, as you may be able to tell from my USPS vehicle model collection:

(insert image of collection here)

Or my stamp collection:

(insert image of stamp collection here)

What wasn't my thing however, was being in retail stores... Or any public space with other people to be honest. I decided I'd just dive in head first regardless and hope exposure therapy would work it's magic. I've been able to get over certain aspects of my anxiety through relentless exposure therapy before, so I figured it might be good for me (even if it's painful).

My first few days working there I had a really hard time focusing, there was just too much going on at once for my mind to process it all. The first few nights after work I had the most vivid dreams I had ever had in my life. They were so realistic I had a hard time distinguishing them from reality, to the point that I'd wake up my partner from talking in my sleep. It was pretty difficult to deal with and keep up, but pushed through knowing I have savings to fall back on if it doesn't work out. Plus, I only really plan to work there temporarily for around 6-12 months anyway!

(insert 30 months later graphic here)

Well, it's been 2 and a half years, and I'm still here. The exposure therapy has worked some magic! I don't panic much anymore, I am much better hooked into queues of when I need to step away from a situation, and I can handle much more than I could before starting the job. I consider myself quite good at my job too! Unexpectedly, it's turned out to be quite knowledge dependent and demanding of and ability to self-teach. I love any job where I get to feel like I'm advancing some sort of repository of personal knowledge day to day! (even if, some of it was due to the owner not providing proper training...). It also ended up requiring the ability to be civil and keep unreasonable people calm (both customers... and the owner). Anyway, dealing with all of this is a huge deal-breaker for most people. On average, the store has a complete team turnover every 6 months or so. It's pretty rare for anyone to stick around here for over a year.

The whole time I've worked here, it's been quite painful. A lot of the pain has been a necessary evil in order to advance my progress with anxiety, but over the past year or so much of the pain hasn't felt like it's come with any reward of progress. Not only that, but things are actually getting worse the more I work. Stress from work has started causing completely disabling stomach aches